ban pitfall-config-by-grep warn *.sh,*.bash,!tests/* '(^|[^[:alnum:]_])grep[[:space:]].*(gitconfig|git/config|config\.worktree)' unless '^[[:space:]]*#|^([^"]*"[^"]*")*[^"]*"(([^"$]|[$][^"(])*[[:space:]])?grep[[:space:]]|^([^'']*''[^'']*'')*[^'']*''[^'']*grep[[:space:]]' -- Ask git for the value it applies (git config --get, with -C DIR or --global --includes): grep reads only the file it names, not the includes or worktree config git follows.
# pitfall: An error path must keep what the success path records. -> none: Describes an error path's bookkeeping; no literal signature on one line.
# pitfall: A function's status is its last command's, including a group that writes a file. -> none: Group exit status is behaviour across lines; no one-line signature.
# pitfall: Text the PR's own code writes is untrusted, and it travels. -> none: Describes untrusted PR text passed on; no literal signature.
# pitfall: A wait on "all checks" includes the check that waits on you. -> pitfall-wait-all-checks
# pitfall: An error message matched as text can be a different error. -> none: 'not found' matches are correct after a gh check; too broad.
# pitfall: A repo-local setting can silently turn a global layer off. -> none: Describes a repo setting overriding a global layer; no one-line signature.
# pitfall: A setting read file by file misses the precedence git applies. -> pitfall-config-by-grep
# pitfall: A notice about a setting must read the file the loop reads. -> none: Describes which file a notice reads; no literal signature.
# pitfall: A flag matched as a word of a command line can be another option's argument. -> none: Checker design; the grep -P case is already gnu-grep-p.
# pitfall: A verdict checked first hides a later state. -> none: Order of verdict checks across lines; no single-line signature.
# pitfall: A limit an agent can lift from its own environment is not a limit. -> none: Who may set a cap is design; env-var limits are often correct.
# pitfall: A deny matched on a file's name also blocks the work done on that file. -> none: Describes a deny's match target; no literal one-line signature.
# pitfall: Reading the same API endpoint twice in one run. -> none: Needs two lines compared; gh api calls are routine.
# pitfall: A filter on a value the repo can change, with an empty result taken as an answer. -> none: Literal label filters are common; the bug needs the label's source.
# pitfall: A cleanup keyed on one record misses what that record no longer points to. -> none: Describes a cleanup's coverage across records; no single-line signature.
# pitfall: `git status --porcelain` does not list ignored files. -> none: Plain status is correct for tracked changes; the sweep reads --ignored.
# pitfall: A script that both computes and publishes a value publishes under the wrong identity when run by hand. -> none: Publish guard is on other lines; a lone statuses call is correct.
# pitfall: `pgrep -f` matches the command line of the shell running it. -> none: a Bash tool command, which lint.sh never reads
# pitfall: awk reads an operand `name=value` as an assignment, not a file. -> none: the operand is a shell variable in any script, and the bug is a missing `./`, which no one line shows.
